WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.266
Most guides open the graph view, say it looks like a brain, and move on.

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:07.484
But the graph is not decoration.

00:00:08.684 --> 00:00:12.098
Pages are nodes, wikilinks are edges.

00:00:12.198 --> 00:00:17.820
Four terms are enough: degree, path length, clustering, components.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:22.504
Two related ideas eight hops apart will never be connected in an answer.

00:00:23.054 --> 00:00:28.877
The global graph is a hairball. The local graph does the work: open a page,

00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:30.404
set depth to two.

00:00:30.954 --> 00:00:36.180
A plain link says two pages are related, not how. Five typed links:

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:41.784
supports, contradicts, extends, part of, and applies.

00:00:41.884 --> 00:00:44.122
Type only about one in five.

00:00:44.672 --> 00:00:49.813
Embeddings are good at recall. A graph is good at multi-hop questions.

00:00:49.863 --> 00:00:51.590
Our questions are relational.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.269
Four numbers, checked monthly. Orphan rate, under five percent.

00:00:57.319 --> 00:01:02.610
Average degree, three to eight. Component count. Stale pages.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:09.550
That's chapter five, graphs. Start with page one: Graph basics for a knowledge base.
